The maximum value of the sum $\frac{a}{2b}+\frac{b}{4c}+\frac{c}{8a}$ can be found using the AM-GM inequality. Since $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are positive real numbers, it follows that

\begin{align*}
\frac{a}{2b}+\frac{b}{4c}+\frac{c}{8a} &= \frac{1}{4}\left(\frac{a}{2b}+\frac{b}{4c}+\frac{c}{8a}\right) \geq \frac{1}{4}\left(\sqrt{\frac{a}{2b}\cdot \frac{b}{4c}\cdot \frac{c}{8a}}\right) \\
&= \frac{1}{4}\cdot\frac{1}{8} = \frac{1}{32}.
\end{align*}Equality occurs when $a=2b=4c,$ or $a=1, b=2, \text{ and } c=4.$ Therefore, the minimum value of the sum is $\boxed{\frac{1}{32}}.$. The answer is \frac{1}{32}
